<article class="post-body">

<!-- §01 — The Drift Problem -->
<section id="s-drift" data-code="drift">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Drift Problem</div>
<h2>Two Minutes In, the Avatar Forgets Its Face</h2>
<p>Modern diffusion-based streaming avatar generators achieve impressive results for short clips. The standard approach distills a bidirectional diffusion transformer into a causal autoregressive architecture, then generates video chunk by chunk: each new chunk attends to a handful of previously generated chunks plus the original reference image.</p>
<p>The flaw is immediate: <strong>the receptive field is bounded</strong>. A model that sees only the last 3–5 generated chunks has no way to verify that the avatar's shirt color at second 45 matches the reference. Small errors in each chunk compound. The avatar's face shifts in lighting; the watch it was supposed to wear disappears; the background color drifts. This is <em>temporal drift</em>.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">temporal drift — simulated streaming generation over 60 seconds</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">animates automatically</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="drift-canvas" width="640" height="210" style="width:100%;max-width:640px;display:block;margin:0 auto;border-radius:8px"></canvas>
    <div class="drift-legend">
      <span><span class="drift-legend-dot" style="background:#ef4444"></span> No memory (causal only)</span>
      <span><span class="drift-legend-dot" style="background:#8b5cf6"></span> InteractiveAvatar (LSVM)</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>A second, orthogonal problem: these streaming methods are fundamentally <em>audio-driven</em>. The avatar can lip-sync to audio. But if you ask it to "pick up a phone and tell me the time," it generates speech for the response — but the body just stands there, or produces random audio-correlated gestures. There is no explicit model of <em>what the user wants the avatar to do</em>.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Two root causes</div>
  <p><strong>Visual consistency:</strong> causal streaming sees only recent chunks — no global memory of appearance.<br>
  <strong>Intent understanding:</strong> audio-driven approaches respond to sound, not meaning — actions are learned from coarse audio-motion correlations, not intent.</p>
</div>
</section>

<!-- §02 — LSVM -->
<section id="s-lsvm" data-code="lsvm">
<div class="section-no">02 — Long-Short Visual Memory</div>
<h2>Two Kinds of Forgetting, Two Kinds of Memory</h2>
<p>InteractiveAvatar addresses temporal drift with <strong>Long-Short Visual Memory (LSVM)</strong> — a dual-buffer architecture that keeps both recent detail and long-range global appearance.</p>
<p>The idea: do not attempt to keep every frame. Instead, compress each generated frame into a compact memory token, and maintain two distinct stores:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Short-term memory M<sub>s</sub></strong> — a FIFO queue of the last K frames (≈ 5 seconds). Preserves local continuity and ensures smooth chunk transitions.</li>
  <li><strong>Long-term memory M<sub>l</sub></strong> — a fixed-capacity buffer of N semantically diverse frames selected from the full generation history. Prevents global drift by anchoring the model to representative visual states.</li>
</ul>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">LSVM architecture — dual memory buffers feed into the DiT</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">hover a component for details</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="overflow-x:auto">
    <svg id="lsvm-svg" viewBox="0 0 680 280" style="width:100%;min-width:560px;font-family:var(--ff-mono)" role="img" aria-label="LSVM architecture diagram">
      <!-- Input frames -->
      <g id="lsvm-input">
        <rect x="10" y="110" width="70" height="60" rx="6" fill="rgba(139,92,246,.15)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <text x="45" y="134" font-size="9" fill="#8b5cf6" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">VIDEO</text>
        <text x="45" y="147" font-size="9" fill="#8b5cf6" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">FRAMES</text>
        <text x="45" y="160" font-size="8" fill="#6b7280" text-anchor="middle">z_t</text>
      </g>
      <!-- Arrow to compressor -->
      <line x1="80" y1="140" x2="112" y2="140" stroke="#6b7280" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>
      <!-- Compressor -->
      <g id="lsvm-compress">
        <rect x="112" y="108" width="76" height="64" rx="6" fill="rgba(6,182,212,.12)" stroke="#06b6d4" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <text x="150" y="132" font-size="9" fill="#06b6d4" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">3DConv</text>
