<p>A lightweight compression model C(·) converts video latents into compact tokens. At each step, the short-term memory ingests the newest token and pops the oldest — which then gets evaluated by the Dynamic Key-Frame Selection strategy (§03) for possible promotion into long-term memory.</p>

<p>During training, the model is forced to reconstruct frames from both short-term and long-range positions — building balanced encoding that captures fine-grained local detail and global semantic state simultaneously.</p>

<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">Combined history representation</div>
<span class="math-var">Φ(H)</span> = Concat(<span class="math-var">M_l</span>, <span class="math-var">M_s</span>)<br>
<span class="math-var">M_s</span> = &#123;<span class="math-var">m</span><sub>t-K+1</sub>, …, <span class="math-var">m</span><sub>t</sub>&#125;  <span class="math-comment">← last K ≈ 5s</span><br>
<span class="math-var">M_l</span> = &#123;<span class="math-var">m̃</span><sub>1</sub>, …, <span class="math-var">m̃</span><sub>N</sub>&#125;  <span class="math-comment">← N diverse long-range frames</span><br>
<span class="math-var">m_t</span> = C(<span class="math-var">z_t</span>)  <span class="math-comment">← compression model (3DConv + attention)</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- §03 — DKFS -->
<section id="s-dkfs" data-code="dkfs">
<div class="section-no">03 — Dynamic Key-Frame Selection</div>
<h2>Only Diverse Frames Earn a Permanent Slot</h2>
<p>Long-term memory has fixed capacity N. The hard question: which frames should occupy those slots? Uniform time sampling wastes slots on near-duplicate frames from a static scene. Instead, InteractiveAvatar uses <strong>Dynamic Key-Frame Selection (DKFS)</strong> to greedily maximize <em>semantic diversity</em> in the long-term buffer.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">DKFS — four-step decision process for each popped frame</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">use Prev / Next to walk through steps</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="dkfs-steps">
      <div class="step-box active" data-i="0">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Frame exits short-term FIFO</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The oldest token m<sub>out</sub> is popped from M<sub>s</sub>. Its associated real image frames (the original pixel frames, not the compressed token) are retrieved from cache.</div>
          <div class="step-code">m_out ← Pop(M_s)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-i="1">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">SigLIP2 semantic feature extraction</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The real frames are fed through SigLIP2 — a multilingual vision-language encoder — to produce a compact semantic descriptor vector s<sub>cand</sub>. This captures <em>what the scene looks like</em>, not just pixel values.</div>
          <div class="step-code">s_cand = mean([SigLIP2(I_j) for j in frames])</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-i="2">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Compute global redundancy score R</div>
          <div class="step-desc">For each existing slot s<sub>i</sub> in M<sub>l</sub>, compute its mean cosine similarity to all other slots. Average across all slots to get R. Then simulate: if we replace the most similar slot with s<sub>cand</sub>, what would R' be?</div>
          <div class="step-code">ρ_i = mean(cos(s_i, s_j) for j≠i)
R = mean(ρ_i for all i)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-i="3">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Accept if diversity improves</div>
          <div class="step-desc">If R′ &lt; R (the candidate would reduce average redundancy), insert it into M<sub>l</sub>, evicting the most redundant existing slot. Otherwise, discard. The buffer stays diverse — only novel-content frames survive.</div>
          <div class="step-code">if R' &lt; R:
    M_l.insert(m_cand)   # ← diverse enough
else:
    forget(m_cand)       # ← redundant</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="dkfs-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <button id="dkfs-next">Next →</button>
      <span class="step-prog" id="dkfs-prog">Step 1 of 4</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Ablation confirms each piece: removing long-term memory entirely drops object consistency OBJ from 85.2 → 78.4 (−6.8 pp). Replacing DKFS with uniform random sampling causes visible watch-face distortions. DKFS isn't just a heuristic — it's the mechanism that keeps the long-term buffer from filling with near-duplicate "standing still" frames.</p>

<div class="callout cyan">
  <div class="callout-title">Concrete example</div>
  <p>User asks the avatar to sit on a sofa and pick up a phone, then later stand and wave. Without long-term memory, chunk 30 has no record that the avatar sat down. With DKFS, the "sitting on sofa" state was flagged as semantically novel (low cosine similarity to the standing reference frame) and inserted into M<sub>l</sub> — so chunk 30 can still reference it.</p>
</div>
</section>

<!-- §04 — RRM -->
<section id="s-rrm" data-code="rrm">
<div class="section-no">04 — Reasoning-Reaction Module</div>
<h2>LLM → Intent → Coordinated Action + Speech</h2>
<p>Even a perfectly consistent avatar is useless if it can't understand what you're asking. InteractiveAvatar's <strong>Reasoning-Reaction Module (RRM)</strong> routes user speech through an ASR → LLM pipeline that produces three outputs simultaneously:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Action prompt p<sub>act</sub></strong> — natural-language description of the physical motion to execute (e.g., "He reaches for the watch on his wrist and looks at it")</li>
  <li><strong>Audio response a<sub>resp</sub></strong> — the spoken reply, to be lip-synced during generation</li>
  <li><strong>Stable-state prompt p<sub>stable</sub></strong> — the static visual condition after the action completes ("A man standing calmly, wearing a watch")</li>
</ul>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">State-Cycling — the interaction loop</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">animates automatically</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="state-machine" id="state-machine">
      <div class="sm-state sm-listen" id="sm-listen">
        <div class="sm-state-icon">🎤</div>
        <div class="sm-state-label">Listening</div>
      </div>
      <div class="sm-arrow">→</div>
      <div class="sm-state sm-reason" id="sm-reason">
        <div class="sm-state-icon">🧠</div>
        <div class="sm-state-label">LLM Reason</div>
      </div>
      <div class="sm-arrow">→</div>
      <div class="sm-state sm-action" id="sm-action">
        <div class="sm-state-icon">🎬</div>
        <div class="sm-state-label">Action State</div>
      </div>
      <div class="sm-arrow">→</div>
      <div class="sm-state sm-stable" id="sm-stable">
        <div class="sm-state-icon">😌</div>
        <div class="sm-state-label">Stable State</div>
      </div>
      <div class="sm-arrow">↩</div>
    </div>
    <div class="sm-desc" id="sm-desc">Avatar waits for user speech input. Audio is passed to ASR → text.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>State-Cycling prevents motion drift</h3>
<p>Without State-Cycling, the model would receive only the action prompt continuously — causing it to endlessly loop the same action. Instead, State-Cycling has two phases:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Action phase:</strong> model is conditioned on (p<sub>act</sub>, a<sub>resp</sub>). Audio drives lip sync; action prompt guides body pose.</li>
  <li><strong>Stable phase:</strong> once a<sub>resp</sub> finishes, audio condition is cleared and the model switches to p<sub>stable</sub>. Avatar holds still — no spurious motion drift.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Cache-Switching reduces response latency</h3>
<p>When a new action prompt arrives, the previously cached KV tensors for adjacent chunks are stale — they were computed under the old prompt. Recomputing from scratch is expensive. Instead, <strong>Cache-Switching</strong> surgically replaces only the prompt-conditioned portions of the KV cache: the adjacent chunk keys/values are recomputed under the new prompt, while all other cached computation is reused. This reduces the perceived latency between a user command and the start of the new action.</p>

<div class="callout green">
  <div class="callout-title">Cache-Switching in practice</div>
  <p>Ablation: disabling Cache-Switching (w/o CacheSwitching) produces visibly delayed action onset — e.g., picking up a book appears noticeably sluggish. Disabling the full RRM (w/o RRM) causes the avatar to ignore body actions entirely, producing only verbal responses.</p>
</div>
</section>

